      "panel-about": {
        "kind": "Panel",
        "spec": {
          "data": {
            "kind": "QueryGroup",
            "spec": {
              "queries": [],
              "queryOptions": {},
              "transformations": []
            }
          },
          "description": "",
          "id": 8,
          "links": [],
          "title": "About the data",
          "vizConfig": {
            "group": "text",
            "kind": "VizConfig",
            "spec": {
              "fieldConfig": {
                "defaults": {},
                "overrides": []
              },
              "options": {
                "code": {
                  "language": "plaintext",
                  "showLineNumbers": false,
                  "showMiniMap": false
                },
                "content": "**What it shows.** One drone flight over Government Point and Percos Beach, in the Jack and Laura Dangermond Preserve at Point Conception, California, turned into two kinds of 3D scene from the same photographs: an integrated mesh on the left of each curtain and Gaussian splats on its right. Drag the curtain to move between them. The three maps share nothing but the flight: each has its camera, and in each the curtain's two sides move together. Drag with the right button (or Ctrl and drag) to tilt and turn.\n\n**A mesh and splats.** The *integrated mesh* is one continuous surface of triangles with the photographs laid on it as a texture, a single skin over the ground, the shrubs and the cliff. The *Gaussian splats* are no surface at all: millions of small, soft, coloured ellipsoids, each with its own place, size, turn and opacity, fitted so that, blended together from any side, they look like the photographs. So the mesh is what you measure on: it has a surface to cut, to cast shadows from, to stand things on. The splats are what you look at: thin things (shrub branches, fence wires, the spray on the waves), water and the cliff's crumbling edge come out as the photographs saw them, where the mesh smooths them into its skin or melts them into sheets. Close up, the mesh's texture blurs into its triangles; the splats keep their grain until their ellipses show, and the ground seen edge-on thins away.\n\n**Where they come from.** The Nature Conservancy flew the preserve with Esri, Wingtra and UC Santa Barbara and publishes five products of this flight on ArcGIS Online (the table); these maps draw two, streamed from ArcGIS by your browser: [the Gaussian splats](https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=27cffcafffc2460b88bb7f21afd75df6), OGC 3D Tiles 1.1 with the Khronos extension `KHR_gaussian_splatting`, and [the integrated mesh](https://www.arcgis.com/home/item.html?id=a957491d210642f7959b1b50c57ed7fa), an I3S scene layer, each at several levels of detail of which only the tiles in view are read. Their terms: *Data and derived products are provided for demonstration, research, educational, visualization, and analytical purposes. Copyright © The Nature Conservancy. All rights reserved. Source: The Nature Conservancy.*\n\n**How it is drawn.** By Kepler Plus: the splats by its Gaussian Splats layer, set on the map's ground by their lowest splats; the mesh by the free panel's 3D tile layer, at the heights it was surveyed at, a few metres over the sea like the splats once grounded. The satellite picture under the whole site is today's, not the flight's; the close-ups lie on a dark map, as satellite pictures stop short of the sea at that zoom.",
                "mode": "markdown"
              }
            },
            "version": "13.3.0"
          }
        }
      },
